 "L6.1": {"established": "yes", "quote": "there is a unique $n$ with $a_n<x<a_{n+1}$. By definition $a_{n+1}$ is the smallest integer $>a_n$ in $S_n:=\\{m:\\gcd(m,a_i)>1\\ \\forall i\\le n\\}$. But $x\\in S_\\infty\\subseteq S_n$ and $x>a_n$, so $a_{n+1}\\le x$ — contradiction.", "note": "Both inclusions (terms ⊆ S∞, S∞∩[a₁,∞) ⊆ terms) proved in Step 1."},
 "L6.2": {"established": "yes", "quote": "The family $\\mathcal F_{s+1}$ is infinite; pick $e\\in\\mathcal F_{s+1}$, $e\\neq T$. Then $e\\supseteq I\\cup Z_{s+1}\\supseteq I\\cup Z_s\\supseteq T$, so $e\\supsetneq T$, contradicting (i).", "note": "Finiteness proved for all cases via self-dual intersecting-antichain theorem (Steps 3–4), an equivalent route; membership then depends only on primes dividing L."},
 "L6.3": {"established": "yes", "quote": "Since each $h_i\\mid L$, for every positive integer $m$: $m\\in S_\\infty\\iff$ some $h_i\\mid m\\iff$ some $h_i\\mid m+L\\iff m+L\\in S_\\infty$. Let $T:=\\#\\bigl(S_\\infty\\cap[a_1,a_1+L)\\bigr)$; since $a_1\\in S_\\infty$ (Step 1), $T\\ge 1$.", "note": "Membership invariant under m→m+L for all m≥1; T = window count; ends with a_{n+T}=a_n+L for every n≥1."}
